  "output": "Now I have enough to give a thorough analysis. This is a pure Python backend — no React — so I'll skip the re-render section and focus on what's actually present.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\n### 1. N+1 Query Pattern — `save_policy_triples` (db.py:466–502)\n\n**Worst issue in the codebase.** For every triple in a batch, three separate `await db.execute()` calls are issued inside a Python loop: the triple insert, a subject node upsert, and an object node upsert. For 100 triples that's ~300 round-trips before the single final `commit`.\n\n```python\n# Current (O(3N) awaited DB ops)\nfor triple in triples:\n    await db.execute(\"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO policy_triples ...\", ...)\n    subject_node_id = await self._upsert_node(db, triple.subject, ...)\n    object_node_id  = await self._upsert_node(db, triple.object_, ...)\n    await db.execute(\"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO graph_edges ...\", ...)\nawait db.commit()\n```\n\n**Fix:** use `executemany` for the bulk inserts and de-duplicate node upserts in memory:\n\n```python\nasync def save_policy_triples(self, triples: list[PolicyTriple]) -> None:\n    # Build a local entity→node_id cache to skip redundant upserts\n    node_cache: dict[str, str] = {}\n\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        # Batch-insert all triples at once\n        await db.executemany(\n            \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO policy_triples (...) VALUES (...)\",\n            [self._triple_params(t) for t in triples],\n        )\n\n        # Collect unique entities, upsert each only once\n        for triple in triples:\n            for entity in (triple.subject, triple.object_):\n                if entity not in node_cache:\n                    node_cache[entity] = await self._upsert_node(db, entity, None, triple.source_url)\n\n        # Batch-insert edges\n        edge_params = [\n            (\n                f\"{t.triple_id}:edge\",\n                node_cache[t.subject],\n                t.predicate_lemma,\n                node_cache[t.object_],\n                t.triple_id,\n                2.0 if \"CROSS_INSTRUMENT_CORROBORATION\" in t.validation_flags else 1.0,\n            )\n            for t in triples\n        ]\n        await db.executemany(\n            \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO graph_edges (...) VALUES (...)\",\n            edge_params,\n        )\n        await db.commit()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. N+1 DB Writes — `_save_teacher_notes` (specialist.py:182–200)\n\nEach flagged triple opens a brand-new `aiosqlite` connection and commits individually via `save_rule_refinement`. For 10 flagged triples that's 10 connections opened, 10 commits, 10 connections closed.\n\n```python\n# Current — one connection + commit per triple\nfor triple in flagged:\n    await self._db.save_rule_refinement(...)   # opens conn, commits, closes conn\n```\n\n**Fix:** add a batch method to `DatabaseManager`:\n\n```python\n# db.py — new method\nasync def save_rule_refinements_batch(self, records: list[dict]) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        await db.executemany(\n            \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO rule_refinements \"\n            \"(refinement_id, triple_id, validation_flag, llm_correction, correction_type, applied_to_rule) \"\n            \"VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n            [\n                (r[\"refinement_id\"], r[\"triple_id\"], r[\"validation_flag\"],\n                 r[\"llm_correction\"], r[\"correction_type\"], r[\"applied_to_rule\"])\n                for r in records\n            ],\n        )\n        await db.commit()\n\n# specialist.py — updated _save_teacher_notes\nasync def _save_teacher_notes(self, triples, raw):\n    if not self._config.parsing.teacher_loop_enabled:\n        return\n    flagged = [t for t in triples if t.validation_flags]\n    if not flagged:\n        return\n    try:\n        records = [\n            dict(refinement_id=str(uuid.uuid4()), triple_id=t.triple_id,\n                 validation_flag=\",\".join(t.validation_flags),\n                 llm_correction=raw[:1000], correction_type=\"nuance\", applied_to_rule=None)\n            for t in flagged\n        ]\n        await self._db.save_rule_refinements_batch(records)\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.warning(\"Teacher-loop note save failed for %s: %s\", self._agent_id, e)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Connection-Per-Operation Pattern (db.py — everywhere)\n\nEvery `DatabaseManager` method does `async with aiosqlite.connect(...)`. On a query with 8 specialists each calling `_try_graph_retrieval → search_policy_triples`, plus orchestrator saves, you open and close ~20+ SQLite connections per request. SQLite connection setup isn't free (file open, shared cache negotiation, WAL check).\n\n**Fix:** hold a single persistent connection with WAL mode:\n\n```python\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path=_DB_PATH):\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._conn: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def initialize(self) -> None:\n        self._conn = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n        await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n        await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\n        self._conn.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n        await self._conn.executescript(_SCHEMA)\n        await self._conn.commit()\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._conn:\n            await self._conn.close()\n            self._conn = None\n\n    # Replace `async with aiosqlite.connect(...)` throughout with self._conn\n```\n\nMake the singleton `DatabaseManager` initialized once at app startup (FastAPI lifespan), then reuse the same connection. This alone will reduce per-query overhead significantly given SQLite's lightweight locking model.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Regex Recompiled on Every URL Fetch (engine.py:72–77)\n\n`_normalize_url` calls `re.search(r'...', url)` — the pattern string is recompiled into a regex object on every single URL fetch. Python caches the last few `re.compile` results internally, but explicit compilation is cleaner and guaranteed:\n\n```python\n# Current\nimport re\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    if re.search(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$', url):\n        ...\n\n# Fix — module-level constant\n_LAWS_ACT_RE = re.compile(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$')\n\nclass FetchEngine:\n    def _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n        if _LAWS_ACT_RE.search(url):\n            return url.rstrip('/') + '/FullText.html'\n        return url\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Policy Registry Read 8× on Startup (specialist.py:78–81)\n\n`_load_instruments` opens and parses `policy_registry.json` once per `SpecialistAgent` instantiation. With 8 agents, the file is read and JSON-decoded 8 times at startup. Since all 8 agents are singletons this is a one-time cost, but it's still wasteful.\n\n```python\n# Fix — module-level cache\n_REGISTRY_CACHE: list[dict] | None = None\n\ndef _get_registry() -> list[dict]:\n    global _REGISTRY_CACHE\n    if _REGISTRY_CACHE is None:\n        with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n            _REGISTRY_CACHE = json.load(f)\n    return _REGISTRY_CACHE\n\nclass SpecialistAgent:\n    def _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n        return [r for r in _get_registry() if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. Orphaned Background Tasks (engine.py:185–198)\n\n`asyncio.create_task(run_parser())` fires background parsing tasks with no reference held. Python will warn about these at GC time, and on app shutdown they may be interrupted mid-write:\n\n```python\n# Current\nasyncio.create_task(run_parser())   # reference dropped immediately\n\n# Fix — track tasks, cancel on shutdown\nclass FetchEngine:\n    def __init__(self, ...):\n        ...\n        self._bg_tasks: set[asyncio.Task] = set()\n\n    def _schedule_deterministic_parsing(self, url, content):\n        if not self._config.parsing.enabled:\n            return\n        async def run_parser():\n            try:\n                await get_sage_pipeline().process_document_for_url(url, content)\n            except Exception as e:\n                logger.warning(\"Deterministic parsing failed for %s: %s\", url, e)\n\n        task = asyncio.create_task(run_parser())\n        self._bg_tasks.add(task)\n        task.add_done_callback(self._bg_tasks.discard)  # auto-cleanup on completion\n\n    async def close(self):\n        if self._bg_tasks:\n            await asyncio.gather(*self._bg_tasks, return_exceptions=True)\n        await self._client.aclose()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. Sequential COUNT Queries in `get_coverage_stats` (db.py:557–568)\n\nFour `COUNT(*)` calls run sequentially on the same connection. Run them concurrently or combine into a single query:\n\n```python\n# Fix — one round-trip instead of four\nasync def get_coverage_stats(self) -> dict:\n    sql = \"\"\"\n        SELECT\n          (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM kiss_documents)  AS documents,\n          (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM policy_triples)  AS triples,\n          (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM graph_nodes)     AS nodes,\n          (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM graph_edges)     AS edges\n    \"\"\"\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        async with db.execute(sql) as cursor:\n            row = await cursor.fetchone()\n    return {\"documents\": row[0], \"triples\": row[1], \"nodes\": row[2], \"edges\": row[3]}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. `db.initialize()` Called on Every Teacher-Loop Save (specialist.py:189)\n\n`self._db.initialize()` runs the full `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` schema script on every call to `_save_teacher_notes`. Even with `IF NOT EXISTS`, this is wasted work every query that produces flagged triples. Call `initialize()` once at app startup, not inside a hot path.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Full-Table LIKE Scans Bypass Indexes (db.py:519–526)\n\n`search_policy_triples` uses `lower(subject) LIKE ?` patterns. The existing index `idx_triples_subject` on `subject` **will not** be used because the query applies `lower()` and a leading-wildcard `%...%` pattern. This degrades to a full table scan on every query.\n\n**Fix options (in order of effectiveness):**\n1. Add a [SQLite FTS5 virtual table](https://www.sqlite.org/fts5.html) over `sentence_text`, `subject`, and `object_`. FTS5 gives full-text inverted-index search and handles case-insensitivity natively.\n2. Add a generated/stored lowercased column and index it: `subject_lower TEXT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (lower(subject)) STORED` with an index on it — eliminates the function call while keeping LIKE.\n3. At minimum, avoid the leading wildcard when the query entity can anchor at a word boundary.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Priority Table\n\n| # | Issue | File | Impact | Fix Complexity |\n|---|-------|------|--------|----------------|\n| 1 | N+1 inserts in `save_policy_triples` | db.py:466 | High | Low |\n| 2 | N+1 connections in `_save_teacher_notes` | specialist.py:182 | Medium | Low |\n| 3 | Connection-per-operation | db.py (all) | High at scale | Medium |\n| 4 | Regex recompile per URL | engine.py:72 | Low | Trivial |\n| 5 | JSON registry read ×8 | specialist.py:78 | Low | Trivial |\n| 6 | Orphaned background tasks | engine.py:185 | Medium (shutdown) | Low |\n| 7 | Sequential COUNT queries | db.py:557 | Low | Trivial |\n| 8 | `initialize()` in hot path | specialist.py:189 | Low | Trivial |\n| 9 | Full-table LIKE scans | db.py:519 | High at scale | Medium–High |\n\nItems 1, 2, and 3 give the biggest return for effort. Items 4, 5, 7, 8 are single-line fixes worth doing immediately.\n",
